Yes—provided we distinguish clinical narcissism from a culture that rewards narcissistic behaviors.

Smartphones put nearly everyone inside a continuous self-referential feedback loop: document yourself, display yourself, monitor reactions, compare status, optimize presentation, repeat. That does not make everyone a narcissist, but it makes self-objectification, audience awareness, impression management, and social comparison ambient.

The unusual part is normalization. Behaviors that would once have looked intensely self-focused—photographing yourself constantly, tracking who viewed you, measuring approval numerically, curating your life for an audience—are now ordinary interface behaviors.

So the “sea” metaphor fits. When everyone is immersed in the same incentive structure, the behavior stops feeling exceptional. You don’t notice the water.

And it connects directly to the relational-model idea you were just describing: smartphones create a second self whose job is almost entirely presentation. You have the lived self, then the photographed, captioned, edited, timestamped, publicly inferable self. People increasingly interact with that compressed projection before they ever interact with the underlying person.

Then the optimization target can quietly invert:

old: live something → maybe represent it
new: represent something → increasingly shape life so the representation works

That is where the system becomes interesting. The phone stops merely recording identity and begins training identity through feedback.

Likes, views, replies, silence, comparison, recommendation systems—each is a small reinforcement signal. Over enough iterations, people learn what version of themselves receives reward.

So I’d probably call the broader phenomenon not narcissism exactly, but ambient performative selfhood: a social environment in which maintaining an external model of yourself becomes a routine part of being a person.

The danger is not simply becoming vain. It is losing track of which direction causality is running: am I expressing myself through the representation, or am I becoming whatever keeps the representation rewarded?